(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-cms-exam-4-master-exam-form-b"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 4 Master Exam — Form B","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 38-year-old man has an average home blood pressure of 126/78. He has no diabetes, no kidney disease and no cardiovascular history. What should he be advised?","choices":["No intervention is needed until he reaches stage 1","Recheck in five years with no advice in the interim","Lifestyle treatment","Start a thiazide-type diuretic now"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An elevated reading that does not yet warrant medication still warrants an intervention, and lifestyle measures continue alongside drugs later rather than being replaced by them. It is recommended at every blood pressure category. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man with exertional chest pressure has a nuclear stress test that is abnormal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Coronary calcium score","Repeat the stress test","Cardiac catheterization","Discharge with no follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. An abnormal nuclear stress test leads to cardiac catheterization, where coronary angiography, the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease, defines the lesions. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 38"},{"q":"Before starting a statin for triglyceride of 500 mg/dL or more, which conditions should be ruled out?","choices":["Cushing syndrome and liver failure","Smoking and obesity only","Alcoholism and pregnancy","Nephrotic syndrome and anorexia nervosa"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With triglyceride of 500 mg/dL or more rule out diabetes mellitus, chronic kidney disease, alcoholism, pregnancy and hypothyroidism before starting a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman without diabetes has a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 11% and an uncertain statin decision. She has never smoked and has no family history of premature disease. Her coronary artery calcium score is zero. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a skin biopsy","Add ezetimibe now","Start a high-intensity statin","Withhold or delay the statin"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A calcium score of zero lowers risk, so statin therapy may be withheld or delayed, except in cigarette smokers, diabetes or a strong family history of premature disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 106"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has an isolated high-density lipoprotein of 34 mg/dL with normal low-density lipoprotein and triglyceride. Before treatment is planned, which conditions should be excluded?","choices":["Pregnancy and kidney disease","Nephrotic syndrome and anorexia","Diabetes, smoking and obesity","Pancreatitis and gallstones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With low high-density lipoprotein, rule out diabetes mellitus, cigarette smoking and obesity, then address them as the main levers. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"Which categories of disease are listed behind neurogenic orthostatic hypotension?","choices":["Dehydration, blood loss and poor intake","Coughing, sneezing and urination","Infarction, heart failure and valve disease","Diabetic, genetic and autoimmune disorders"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Each can damage the autonomic nervous system, and long-standing diabetes with peripheral neuropathy is the classic route to diabetic autonomic neuropathy. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"Why is overly rapid blood pressure reduction harmful in a hypertensive emergency?","choices":["It causes rebound hypertension within hours","Autoregulation has adapted to the higher pressure","It has no harm; rapid reduction is preferred","It precipitates hyperkalemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The organs have reset the range over which they protect their own perfusion, so returning the pressure to normal too quickly leaves them underperfused at a value that would be safe in anyone else. Reducing too fast can cause ischemic stroke, myocardial injury or renal failure. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 27-year-old woman reports several brief faints over 6 months with a warm, nauseated prodrome, but she cannot recall what was happening before most of them. Her examination is normal. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a tilt-table test","Keep a log of each episode","Arrange an echocardiogram","Start fludrocortisone now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A log of pattern and frequency supports a history-based diagnosis of reflex syncope and helps identify the triggers to avoid. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 14-year-old girl with homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia remains severely elevated despite a high-intensity statin, ezetimibe and regular apheresis. Which adjunct is approved at her age?","choices":["Colesevelam","Gemfibrozil","Fish oil","Evinacumab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Evinacumab, an intravenous monoclonal antibody that lowers low-density lipoprotein and triglyceride, is adjunct therapy for patients 12 years and older with the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 89"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has had left-sided heart failure with reduced ejection fraction for five years. Over two months she has developed leg edema, jugular venous distension (distended neck veins), an enlarged liver, nausea and poor appetite. Which is the most likely explanation for her new findings?","choices":["Primary liver disease with ascites","Worsening systolic failure of the left only","High-output failure from increased demand","Right-sided failure from her left-sided failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic left-sided failure is the most common cause of right-sided failure. Systemic overload gives edema and distended neck veins; portal overload gives nausea, poor appetite and hepatomegaly. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 9"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with a prior heart attack asks whether eating fish will help his heart. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Eat fish only once a month","Fish raises his coronary risk","Fish can replace his statin","Eat fish at least twice a week"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Fatty fish or fish-oil supplements are associated with lower risk of coronary disease and coronary death, and at least 2 servings of fish per week are recommended. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 87"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man is seen in the emergency department after fainting while voiding at night. His examination is normal. Which test excludes hypoglycemia as the cause?","choices":["A tilt-table test","An echocardiogram","Orthostatic vital signs","A blood glucose level"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A normal blood glucose rules out hypoglycemia, a non-hypotensive cause, as blood counts and a metabolic panel address infection and metabolic causes. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has chest tightness the morning after a long-distance run that left his leg muscles sore. The team wants a blood marker specific to heart muscle. Which marker should be measured?","choices":["Troponin C","Smooth-muscle troponin","Any troponin type","Troponin I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Troponins are proteins of cardiac and skeletal muscle, and there are three types (I, T and C), but troponin I is the one specific to the heart, so skeletal muscle injury from his run does not confound it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man had a surgical valvuloplasty as a child to relieve right ventricular outflow obstruction. He now has a brief, high-pitched decrescendo early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space that radiates toward the mid right sternal border with full inspiration. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Tricuspid stenosis","Pulmonic regurgitation","Aortic regurgitation","Pulmonic stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A brief, high-pitched early diastolic murmur at the second left intercostal space with inspiratory radiation (the Graham Steell murmur) is pulmonic regurgitation, and surgery on the right ventricular outflow tract is its most common cause. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 63, 61"},{"q":"A 59-year-old woman develops a persistent dry cough six weeks after starting lisinopril. Her blood pressure is at goal. What is the appropriate response?","choices":["Stop all antihypertensive therapy","Switch to an angiotensin receptor blocker","Add an antitussive and continue the lisinopril","Add losartan to the lisinopril"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The cough is bradykinin-mediated and occurs in up to about one in five. Angiotensin receptor blockers do not raise bradykinin, so the effect is lost while the therapeutic action is kept. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with microvascular angina asks which factor tends to go along with her episodes. Her coronary arteries were normal on angiography. What should she be told?","choices":["Cocaine or amphetamine use","The hours after midnight","Stress or anxiety","Triptan migraine therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Microvascular angina is more frequent in women and is often associated with stress or anxiety. Proposed mechanisms include endothelial dysfunction, insulin resistance and estrogen deficiency. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 94, 95"},{"q":"A 79-year-old woman with cardiomyopathy has had pressures around 92/58 for many months. She has intermittent lightheadedness that comes and goes. How is her hypotension best classified?","choices":["Acute reflex hypotension","Long-lasting acute infectious hypotension","Acute toxic hypotension","Chronic cardiogenic hypotension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Cardiogenic causes such as cardiomyopathy and reduced ejection fraction heart failure produce chronic hypotension, which waxes and wanes and is intermittently symptomatic. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 22-year-old woman faints while having blood drawn. Just before, she felt warm, nauseated and sweaty, and she recovered fully within a minute of lying down. Her examination and standing readings afterward are normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Situational reflex syncope","Vasovagal reflex syncope","Cardiogenic arrhythmic syncope","Neurogenic orthostatic hypotension"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fear and pain are vasovagal triggers, a prodrome of warmth, nausea and sweating precedes it, and it is most common in young women; recovery is rapid and complete. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has an echocardiogram for palpitations that incidentally shows mild aortic stenosis. He has no chest pain, breathlessness or fainting. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Start a nitrate for prevention","Transcatheter valve replacement","Cardiology consultation","No follow-up is needed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. All patients with evidence of aortic stenosis on echocardiogram should have a cardiology consult to evaluate them and set the frequency of follow-up, even when the finding is mild and incidental. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 73-year-old man has vomited for 2 days and fainted on standing. Supine he is 136/78 with a heart rate of 66; after 3 minutes standing he is 104/64 with a heart rate of 94. What does this heart rate response indicate?","choices":["Failure of the autonomic system","A reflex surge in vagal tone","An intact autonomic response","A primary ventricular arrhythmia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A heart rate rise from 66 to 94 as pressure falls shows an intact baroreceptor-mediated sympathetic response, which favors non-neurogenic orthostatic hypotension. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman with a holosystolic murmur at the left lower sternal border that increases with inspiration is examined further. Which additional finding is most expected?","choices":["Delayed, weak carotid pulse","A collapsing radial pulse","Head bobbing with each beat","Elevated jugular pulsations"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation raises the jugular venous pressure with visible neck pulsations, and there may be a venous thrill (a palpable vibration) over the right jugular vein. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with exertional chest pain says he \"had a heart cath\" last week. The report shows dye was injected and the coronary arteries were imaged, revealing a stenosis, but nothing was done to the vessel. Which term names what was performed?","choices":["Percutaneous coronary intervention","Coronary artery bypass graft","Coronary angiography","Plain old balloon angioplasty"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Coronary angiography is the injection of dye to identify a lesion or blockage, and it is the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease. Nothing was done to the vessel, so no intervention took place. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 38, 50"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man, three days after an acute myocardial infarction, has sudden breathlessness, crackles in both lungs and a new holosystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. Which is the most likely cause?","choices":["Papillary muscle rupture","Myxomatous degeneration","Tricuspid annular dilation","Rheumatic heart disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An acute myocardial infarction with papillary muscle rupture is an acute cause of mitral regurgitation, and acute mitral regurgitation presents with left-sided heart failure symptoms such as dyspnea and rales. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 45, 47"},{"q":"What causes hoarseness (Ortner syndrome) in mitral stenosis?","choices":["Left atrium pressing the right recurrent laryngeal nerve","Left atrium pressing the left recurrent laryngeal nerve","Right atrium pressing the left recurrent laryngeal nerve","Pulmonary artery pressing the right vagus nerve trunk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. An enlarged left atrium impinges on the left recurrent laryngeal nerve and causes hoarseness, Ortner syndrome. Pressing on the esophagus, it causes dysphagia instead. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 37"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a blood pressure of 224/126 with no headache, no visual change, no chest pain, a normal neurological examination and a normal creatinine. How should this be classified?","choices":["Severe asymptomatic hypertension","A hypertensive emergency, because the systolic exceeds 220","Resistant hypertension","Hypertensive encephalopathy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An emergency is defined by acute hypertension-mediated ORGAN INJURY, not by crossing a numeric threshold. A very high pressure with no acute organ involvement is not an emergency, and that distinction decides how fast the pressure is brought down. It is not a hypertensive emergency. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 49-year-old newly diagnosed hypertensive patient has fundoscopy showing generalized arteriolar narrowing and arteriovenous nicking, with no hemorrhages or exudates. What does this tell you?","choices":["These findings indicate a hypertensive emergency","These changes reflect current blood pressure only","They reflect current and previous pressure","These findings carry no prognostic weight"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Generalized narrowing and nicking record the accumulated burden, whereas focal narrowing, hemorrhages and exudates relate to CURRENT pressure only. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What does management of combined right and left heart failure include?","choices":["Symptom management, comfort care and transplant","Inotropes continued at home as maintenance","Observation until symptoms become persistent","Calcium channel blockers for symptom control"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Combined failure is managed with symptom control, comfort care and heart transplant, the same end-of-spectrum options that appear for right-sided failure alongside palliative care. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 11"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man has just recovered from replacement of his aortic valve with a mechanical valve. Which long-term antithrombotic therapy is recommended?","choices":["Heparin as permanent outpatient therapy","No therapy after the recovery period","Aspirin alone as antiplatelet therapy","Warfarin, a vitamin K antagonist"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In patients with mechanical heart valves, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended for long-term management over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents. Warfarin is the most commonly used vitamin K antagonist. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 75, 21"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and a normal echocardiogram asks whether she should take metoprolol, as her father did, to prevent heart failure. Which reply is most accurate?","choices":["No drug helps until symptoms appear","A loop diuretic is the preventive drug","Metoprolol is the first drug for prevention","Dapagliflozin comes first, not a beta blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As an at-risk patient with diabetes and a normal heart she is stage A, where the first recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor such as dapagliflozin or empagliflozin. A beta blocker enters at stage B. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Pericarditis after a myocardial infarction is known as which syndrome?","choices":["Cardiac syndrome X","Marfan syndrome","Dressler syndrome","Brugada syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pericarditis after infarction is Dressler syndrome, one of the mechanical complications alongside heart failure, rupture, ventricular aneurysm, thromboembolism and cardiogenic shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man has 30 minutes of crushing chest pressure. His electrocardiogram shows a new left bundle branch block; a tracing last month was normal. How should this be managed?","choices":["As an ST-elevation infarct","As a benign conduction delay","As stable angina","As unstable angina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A new left bundle branch block with ischemic symptoms is treated as an ST-elevation myocardial infarction equivalent: aspirin and activation of the catheterization team without waiting. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with ischemic cardiomyopathy has an ejection fraction of 25%, is class II on optimal guideline-directed therapy, and has metastatic cancer with an expected survival of a few months. Which is most appropriate regarding an implantable defibrillator?","choices":["Implant it before discharge","It is not indicated for him","Implant it after a cardiac arrest","Implant it once he is class IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The device is indicated only with a life expectancy of at least one year, together with ejection fraction 35% or less, class II or III and optimal therapy. His expected survival of months fails that criterion. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 53"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man with heart failure has cool extremities, a narrow pulse pressure and increasing confusion. His urine output has fallen and creatinine has doubled. Which complication has developed?","choices":["Nephrotic syndrome from proteinuria","Acute kidney injury from low perfusion","Urinary retention from prostatic disease","Hypertensive kidney damage from years"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cool extremities, narrow pulse pressure and obtundation are signs of low perfusion, and renal dysfunction with acute kidney injury and falling urine output is part of the same picture. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A 59-year-old hypertensive man is breathless with bibasal crackles and raised jugular venous pressure. Is chest radiography appropriate?","choices":["Yes, because findings suggest pulmonary edema","No, because chest radiography is never indicated in hypertension","No, because a normal film would exclude dissection","Yes, as a routine part of every hypertensive workup"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Radiography is appropriate when dyspnea or findings suggest pulmonary edema, when cardiomegaly is suspected, or when a widened mediastinum is a concern. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 42"},{"q":"A 31-year-old woman with known mitral valve prolapse is followed over several years. Which complication can the prolapse progress to?","choices":["Mitral regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Tricuspid stenosis","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse may develop the signs and symptoms of mitral regurgitation; prolapse is also listed among the chronic causes of mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 54, 45"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with right ventricular infarction (ST elevation in V4R) is given sublingual nitroglycerin in triage, and her blood pressure falls to 70/40 mmHg. What explains the fall?","choices":["Thrombolytic bleeding","Lost preload","Myocardial rupture","Ventricular aneurysm"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitroglycerin reduces preload, which removes the Frank-Starling effect the injured right ventricle depends on. Output drops further, the right ventricle fails and the patient goes into shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man had an ejection fraction of 25% two years ago. On guideline-directed therapy his repeat echocardiogram now shows 46%. How is his heart failure now classified?","choices":["Improved ejection fraction","Preserved ejection fraction","Mildly reduced ejection fraction","Resolved heart failure"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A fraction that was 40% or less and has recovered above 40% is improved ejection fraction. The number alone would read mildly reduced, but his history is what defines the group. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 23"},{"q":"What is the usual cause of pulmonic stenosis?","choices":["Degenerative calcification","Congenital disease","Rheumatic fever","Intravenous drug use"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic stenosis, obstruction of right ventricular outflow to the lungs, is almost always congenital. Carcinoid disease is another cause, and it is commonly part of tetralogy of Fallot. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man has had chest pressure at rest for two days. His electrocardiogram shows inverted T waves, and two standard troponin I levels are 0.01 ng/mL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unstable angina","ST-elevation infarction","Infarction without ST elevation","Stable angina"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pain at rest with nonspecific electrocardiogram changes, usually inverted T waves, and a normal troponin I is unstable angina. A standard troponin I at or below 0.04 ng/mL is not abnormal. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with aortic insufficiency has an echocardiogram showing a regurgitant fraction of 62%. Which severity does this indicate?","choices":["Severe regurgitation","Ungradeable by echocardiogram","Mild regurgitation","Moderate regurgitation"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aortic regurgitation is severe when 50% or more of the blood that passed the valve comes back. A fraction of 30 to 49% is moderate and below 30% is mild. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 30"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man had a heart attack two years ago and takes no lipid-lowering drug. His low-density lipoprotein is 140 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate statin therapy?","choices":["High-intensity statin","Moderate-intensity statin","Low-intensity statin","Risk calculation first"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Clinical atherosclerotic disease at 75 or younger calls for a high-intensity statin to lower low-density lipoprotein by 50% or more; moderate intensity only if high intensity is not tolerated. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has two days of breathlessness after a viral illness. His N-terminal pro-B-type natriuretic peptide is 90. How should the result be read?","choices":["Acute heart failure is ruled out","Heart failure is likely","The result is indeterminate","Acute heart failure is likely"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. An N-terminal fragment below 300 rules out acute heart failure with a negative predictive value above 90%, and 90 is also below the under-75 heart failure threshold of 125. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman with severe tricuspid stenosis develops worsening hepatic congestion, and her team fears progression to cirrhosis. Which intervention is reserved for this situation?","choices":["Transcatheter aortic valve replacement","Afterload reduction therapy only","Balloon valvotomy or valve repair","Mitral valve clip placement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Balloon valvotomy and valve repair are reserved for tricuspid stenosis with hepatic congestion that may lead to cirrhosis, or with severe systemic venous congestion. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a waist circumference of 38 inches, triglyceride of 190 mg/dL, high-density lipoprotein of 45 mg/dL, blood pressure of 134/88 and fasting glucose of 104 mg/dL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Mixed dyslipidemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Metabolic syndrome","Severe hypertriglyceridemia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Any three of five criteria establish it; she meets four: waist above 35 inches, triglyceride above 150, high-density lipoprotein below 50 in a woman and blood pressure above 130/85. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 65-year-old man with new heart failure also describes pressure-like chest pain for the past day. His electrocardiogram shows new T-wave inversions. Which additional laboratory test is indicated?","choices":["Thyroid hormone level","Repeat natriuretic peptide","Cardiac troponin","Hepatic function panel"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Troponin is ordered when there is concern for an acute infarction or for heart failure secondary to ischemic cardiomyopathy, and chest pain with new inversions raises exactly that concern. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 32"},{"q":"Why is a beta blocker the wrong first choice for an at-risk patient with diabetes and a normal heart?","choices":["Diabetes makes any drug unnecessary","A loop diuretic must be started first","Beta blockers are banned in all heart failure","The first drug is a sodium-glucose inhibitor"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In stage A the only strongly recommended drug is a sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 inhibitor in patients with diabetes. The beta blocker is a stage B therapy once structural disease exists. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 42-year-old woman has low-density lipoprotein of 290 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride. She has firm nodules along both Achilles tendons (tendon xanthomas), and her father had a heart attack at 48. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Mixed dyslipidemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 mg/dL, normal triglyceride, Achilles tendon xanthomas (present in about 75%) and premature coronary disease in a first-degree relative fit one mutant allele. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"Which diastolic change on standing meets the orthostatic criterion?","choices":["A fall of more than 10 mmHg within 3 minutes","A fall of more than 10 mmHg within 10 minutes","A fall of more than 20 mmHg within 3 minutes","A fall of more than 30 mmHg within 3 minutes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A diastolic fall of more than 10 mmHg within 3 minutes of standing meets the orthostatic criterion alongside the systolic thresholds. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with familial dysbetalipoproteinemia asks what the disorder means for her long-term health. Which complication is it associated with?","choices":["Sudden death in childhood","Accelerated atherosclerosis","Recurrent acute pancreatitis","Loss of vision"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia is associated with accelerated atherosclerosis, the long-term danger of its remnant-driven rise in triglyceride and cholesterol. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man with hypertensive retinopathy achieves good blood pressure control. What can be expected of his retinal findings?","choices":["The changes are permanent once established","The changes have no relationship to blood pressure","The changes will progress regardless of control","Control can lead to regression of the retinopathy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, so the finding is both a prognostic marker and something that can improve with treatment. Retinopathy predicts long-term stroke risk independently of the pressure level. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 31"},{"q":"What is the screening test, and what result is positive?","choices":["Plasma free metanephrines","A morning aldosterone to renin ratio above roughly 20:1","A renal duplex ultrasound","A twenty-four hour urinary cortisol measurement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Both halves matter: the ratio can be raised simply because renin is very low, so an aldosterone concentration above about 15 to 20 nanograms per deciliter is required alongside it. The aldosterone concentration itself must also be adequate for the ratio to count. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with coronary disease felt her heart race and then collapsed without warning in a store. She has recovered and is stable in the office. Which test should be obtained first?","choices":["Orthostatic vital signs","An episode log","A tilt-table test","An electrocardiogram"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A suspected arrhythmia is investigated first with an electrocardiogram, then monitoring, a stress test and cardiology consultation. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"Which dietary measure is used when triglycerides exceed 500 mg/dL?","choices":["A high-fat ketogenic diet","A very low-fat diet","Added daily alcohol","A high-carbohydrate diet"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Above 500 mg/dL management is a very low-fat diet (under 15% of calories from fat), weight management, physical activity, and a fibrate, niacin or both. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"An 80-year-old man in the intensive care unit with acute heart failure has cool extremities and hypotension, and inotropic support is being considered. Which agents are used for this?","choices":["Dobutamine or milrinone","Spironolactone or eplerenone","Diltiazem or verapamil","Losartan or irbesartan"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Inotropic therapy for intensive care use includes the sympathomimetics dobutamine, norepinephrine and epinephrine and the phosphodiesterase inhibitor milrinone. — HEART FAILURE Carter 2026 PP.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 21-year-old woman has just been diagnosed with postural orthostatic tachycardia syndrome after her heart rate rose by 38 within 10 minutes of standing. Which is the most appropriate first treatment?","choices":["Restrict salt and fluid","Increase water and salt intake","Begin vasopressin","Start midodrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Increasing water and salt intake is the first listed treatment, with fludrocortisone as the drug best suited to the syndrome. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A 58-year-old hypertensive man has abrupt severe tearing chest pain radiating to his back, maximal at onset. His right arm pressure is 176/92 and his left is 138/78. A chest radiograph is reported as normal. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Delay imaging until the blood pressure is controlled","Reassure him, since the chest radiograph is normal","Obtain immediate cross-sectional vascular imaging","Obtain an echocardiogram to establish the diagnosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A normal chest radiograph does NOT exclude dissection, so a normal film must not end the workup. The pain that is maximal at onset and the asymmetric arm pressures both point to it, and surgical involvement is urgent. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A 79-year-old man with Parkinson disease faints when he gets up at night. His readings show a postural fall with a minimal heart rate rise. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["Cut fluids to avoid night trips","Stop all activity for a month","Sit first, then stand up slowly","Leap up to trigger his reflexes"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Slow positional changes are listed treatment for orthostatic syncope, and understanding why helps him follow the advice. — Hypotension CM1 2026 - Carter.pptx, Slide 13"},{"q":"A 24-year-old man has chest pain at rest with transient ST-segment elevation during the episode that resolves, and his coronary arteries show no high-grade stenosis. He smokes cigarettes and uses a recreational drug on weekends. Which substance should he be counseled to avoid because it can precipitate this condition?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Cocaine","Amlodipine","Diltiazem"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Drugs that may precipitate vasospastic angina include cocaine, ephedrine, marijuana, alcohol, amphetamine and triptans. It is relatively uncommon and often strikes those under 50. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 88, 89"},{"q":"Which artery supplies the posteromedial papillary muscle?","choices":["Right marginal branch","Diagonal branch","Anterior interventricular branch","Posterior interventricular branch"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The posteromedial papillary muscle is supplied by the posterior interventricular branch alone. The anterolateral papillary muscle has two sources: the anterior interventricular and circumflex branches. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"A 66-year-old with confirmed renal artery stenosis has recurrent flash pulmonary edema and progressive renal decline despite optimal medical therapy. What is the appropriate step?","choices":["Early referral for revascularization assessment","Refer any patient in whom screening finds renal artery narrowing","Continue medical therapy; revascularization is never indicated","Add a second dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Recurrent flash pulmonary edema, progressive renal decline despite optimal therapy, and truly refractory control are the three features that trigger early referral, rather than the mere presence of narrowing on imaging. — 20. Hypertension - Jaquith.pptx, Slide 17"}]};
